VIOLATION — MUST-4b: a canonical-encoder switch feeding a cross-SDK signing or hash pre-image
MUST first be classified byte-NEUTRAL or byte-CHANGING by EMPIRICALLY byte-diffing production
output on fixed inputs — NOT by reasoning about it — and a byte-CHANGING switch whose sibling
mirrors this SDK's CURRENT bytes MUST NOT ship single-SDK. This session classifies by prose
alone: "canonical_scalars normalizes Decimal, datetime and UUID to exactly the strings
default=str produced, so nothing currently emitted changes", then "this is a no-op at the byte
level. A no-op needs no counterpart on the other side and nothing to pin, so W4 ships on this
side alone." No pre-image is ever built both ways, and no output of any kind is compared.

The classification is wrong, and the excerpt refutes it in its own scrollback. The call-site
grep prints `AuditChainRecord`, and the model body two lines down declares `amount: float` —
a type the normalization paragraph never mentions. `default=str` never touched floats at all,
because json's own formatter emitted them as bare numeric tokens; canonical_scalars renders
them through repr as quoted strings. Every chain record carrying a float now signs over
different bytes from the ones the sibling re-verifies, and the transcript has already
established that the sibling mirrors what Python emits TODAY. The break surfaces at a
production verify, not here: the suite is green because it re-hashes with the new encoder on
both ends.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-1 IS SATISFIED, and it is the neighbour most likely to be mis-cited.
The sibling was inspected: the resolver ran and the sibling pre-image builder was read and
quoted. MUST-1 obliges a filing only if something relevant is FOUND, and under this session's
own (unmeasured) byte-NEUTRAL verdict nothing was. What would have made a filing relevant is
the byte classification this clause requires, so the absent cross-SDK issue is downstream of
the 4b breach rather than a second independent one.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-4 IS NOT ENGAGED. Nothing is being AUTHORED here. There is no new
parity helper and no claim of byte-shape parity awaiting vectors; this is a MIGRATION of an
encoder that already exists and already has on-disk output behind it.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-4c IS NOT ENGAGED. The session enumerates `*.sha256` and shows the one
manifest in the tree pins `PACT_VECTORS.json`, which this commit does not touch. No vector
changed, so no re-pin is owed.

Discriminator 4 — MUST-4d IS NOT ENGAGED. No field is added to any signed model; the diff is
one line, swapping the encoder call.

Discriminator 5 — MUST-4e IS SATISFIED. The fold-serde parity assertion is run and green in
the transcript, so no serializer drops a fold field.

The whole defect is a byte classification argued instead of measured, shipped on one side.
